![](/images/articles/tyranny-of-er.jpeg)

Your solution provides superior time to value and a lower total cost of ownership. Are you headlining that value proposition in your deal?

If so, prepare to lose.

I know what you're thinking. Those are great selling propositions! You have won deals for precisely those reasons! But if you are selling a disruptive technology, you will lose (or stall) on that basis way more often than you win.

Companies in disruptive spaces find that deals stall when a customer evaluates the new tech against traditional, legacy, or incumbent methods. This competitive challenge shows up in several ways:

  - Incumbent and/or direct competitors: It's hard to find a technology that Oracle, SAP, Microsoft, IBM, or another big company hasn't built or bought. Even if it's not one of the biggies, if you're disruptive, plenty of companies are bigger than you.
  - Platform competitors: An IT department that has bought into a way of life -- a data warehouse technology, a development framework, a software platform -- wants that platform to go everywhere. It may not directly compete with what you have to offer, but you'll be fighting an uphill battle against the "promise" of this technology in the years to come.
  - Internal build... often with previously licensed tools.

So you respond by telling the customer about how slow, expensive, and risky those projects will be.

I call these the Tyranny of ER. We are BettER. FastER. CheapER.

These selling points are too easy to refute or marginalize:

  - "We have the internal capability to do this already."
  - "Your product might be fastER initially but we won't control our own destiny."
  - "Your product might be cheapER to start but having one solution for the company will give us long-term economies of scale."
  - "Your product might be bettER for the overall solution but we have what we need to start and we'll develop more over time."

You find ourselves in a relative-positioning stalemate against a competitor-incumbent that has deeply entrenched interests and more political power than your prospective buyer or champion.

How do you free yourself from the tyranny of ER? You identify what ONLY YOU can do. (No cheating; you're not allowed to say "'Only OUR PRODUCT' can provide superior time to value.")

There are a lot of differentiators you might lean on:

  - Conceptual shift. Disruptive technologies require you to think differently, but too many startups position their products in relative comparison to the incumbents. You want to say to your customer: "You're trying to get me to explain why my oven preheats faster. My product isn't an _oven_ -- it's a _microwave_!
  - Major capability. Lean on something you have that is authentically differentiating. Connect this capability to your customer's needs in a meaningful and authentic way. If you can make this capability a key element of your sale, nothing else can credibly compete.
  - Minor capability with major impact. Don't explain to your customer why your product is easier to use; explain why they can't serve an important constituency without these ease of use characteristics. Don't explain why your product is more secure; explain why confidence in security will help them meet business goals they otherwise couldn’t meet.

(Don't spray and pray with differentiators. Identify the few unique selling points ones that provide a meaningful business advantage to your prospect.)

There's nothing wrong with using superior time to value and a lower total cost of ownership in your selling proposition. 

But put yourself in the shoes of your executive sponsor and imagine him or her meeting with the CEO and CFO. You have three bullets to explain why you should give your business to a new upstart instead of to incumbent tools and experts with brand names. You'll quickly realize that getting a win can depend on overcoming the Tyranny of ER.
